@h1 Data: records, collections, scalars h1@

Wit lets you declare structured data inline with @@@code #name: value code@@@,
where the value is a @strong record strong@ @@@code { … } code@@@, a @strong
collection strong@ @@@code [ … ] code@@@, or a @strong scalar strong@. This is
the single source of truth that a self-organising document reads from — declare
it once, render it many ways.

@@@pre
#world: { location - Bag End, time - night, storm - true }
pre@@@

@h2 Records h2@

A record is a set of @code key/value code@ fields. The separator is a @strong
spaced hyphen strong@ @code  -  code@ or a @strong colon strong@ — both produce
the same field:

@@@pre
#point: { x - 3, y - 7 }
#point: { x: 3, y: 7 }
pre@@@

(fixtures 09-records/inline-single-field, colon-separator.) A nested value omits
the separator entirely — the brace or bracket is the signal:
@@@code { history { years - 31 } } code@@@ and @@@code { tags [ a, b ] } code@@@
(nested-record).

Fields are separated by a @strong comma or a newline strong@, never by bare
whitespace, so inline and block records are identical:

@@@pre
#report: {
  title: Q3 Review
  quarter: 2024
  final: true
}
pre@@@

A trailing comma is tolerated (trailing-comma) and an empty @@@code { } code@@@ is
legal (empty-record). Multi-word keys are stored verbatim —
@code years at post code@ (multi-word-key) — and multi-word values are trimmed
(multi-word-value).

@h2 The comma-in-value trap h2@

An @strong unquoted comma splits fields strong@, because the parser reads it as
a field boundary:

@@@pre
#cite: { author - Boud, D., year - 2024 }   ~ E_MALFORMED_RECORD
pre@@@

That is @code E_MALFORMED_RECORD code@ — "record field missing key" (fixture
09-records/comma-in-value). Quote the value:

@@@pre
#cite: { author: "Boud, D.", year: 2024 }
pre@@@

A quoted string makes its bytes literal (commas, newlines become content) and
forces a string type; only @code \" code@ and @code \\ code@ are recognised
inside, and an unterminated quote is @code E_UNTERMINATED_STRING code@ (fixtures
quoted-string-value, quoted-string-multi-line). A multi-line record value uses
the @code key: code@ / indented-block shape, terminated by a top-level comma or
the closing brace (block-record-multi-line-value and its
-with-close / -with-comma-terminator siblings).

@h2 Collections h2@

A collection is an ordered list in @code [ … ] code@. Items are separated by a
@strong comma or newline — not whitespace strong@. That distinction trips
everyone once:

@@@pre
#a: [ x y z ]     ~ ONE item, the string "x y z"
#b: [ x, y, z ]   ~ THREE items
pre@@@

(fixtures 10-collections/whitespace-separator, inline-values — both verified.)
Multi-word items are kept whole (multi-word-items); per-item typing is mixed
(mixed-types); collections may hold records inline (inline-records) or in blocks
(multi-line-records, multi-line-values) and may nest (nested-collection); a
trailing comma is tolerated (trailing-comma) and an empty @code [ ] code@ is
legal (empty-collection).

@h2 Scalars are typed eagerly h2@

A bareword value is classified at parse time: @code true code@ / @code false
code@ become boolean, @code null code@ becomes null, a plain integer or decimal
becomes a number, and everything else is a string.

@@@pre
#report: { title - Q3 Review, quarter - 2024, final - true }
pre@@@

Here @code title code@ is a string, @code quarter code@ a number, @code final
code@ a boolean (scalar-types). This classification drives conditionals,
iteration, and interpolation — see
@a |href /docs/guides/typed-scalars/| Type-classified scalars a@.

@h2 The value-block form h2@

A complex value that spans lines or contains nodes is wrapped as a value-block
def: @@@code #name: code@@@ … @@@code !! code@@@. This is why some data
definitions end in @@@code !! code@@@ — the block form lets the value hold
markup a single line could not.

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li Leaving an unquoted comma in a value (it splits fields). li@
@li Expecting @@@code [ a b c ] code@@@ to be three items — it is one. li@
@li Expecting @code .5 code@ or @code 1e3 code@ to be numbers — they are strings
(see Typed scalars). li@
ul@

